Yes. For the **Arduino + 5 IR sensor + L298N line follower** we were discussing, I’d use a **5-sensor weighted-position PID** rather than a simple left-vs-right calculation. This gives a smoother error value and better cornering. Weighted averages are a common approach for PID line followers. ([GitHub][1])

Assuming your sensors are arranged:

`LEFT ← S1 S2 S3 S4 S5 → RIGHT`

the position weights are:

`0   1000   2000   3000   4000`

so the center is **2000**.

### Full Arduino code

```cpp
/*
   5-SENSOR PID LINE FOLLOWER
   Arduino UNO + L298N + 5 IR sensors

   Sensor arrangement:

   LEFT                         RIGHT
    S1     S2     S3     S4     S5
    |      |      |      |      |
    A0     A1     A2     A3     A4

   Position:
    0     1000   2000   3000   4000

   Center = 2000
*/


// ======================================================
// SENSOR PINS
// ======================================================

const int sensorPins[5] = {
  A0, A1, A2, A3, A4
};


// ======================================================
// L298N MOTOR DRIVER PINS
// ======================================================

// LEFT MOTOR
const int ENA = 5;     // PWM
const int IN1 = 7;
const int IN2 = 8;

// RIGHT MOTOR
const int ENB = 6;     // PWM
const int IN3 = 9;
const int IN4 = 10;


// ======================================================
// PID SETTINGS
// ======================================================

// Start with these values.
// You WILL need to tune them for your robot.

float Kp = 0.08;
float Ki = 0.0000;
float Kd = 0.80;


// ======================================================
// MOTOR SETTINGS
// ======================================================

int baseSpeed = 130;

int maxSpeed = 255;
int minSpeed = 0;


// ======================================================
// PID VARIABLES
// ======================================================

float error = 0;
float previousError = 0;

float integral = 0;
float derivative = 0;

float correction = 0;


// ======================================================
// LINE SETTINGS
// ======================================================

// Change this if your IR sensors work opposite.
//
// true  = BLACK line gives HIGH reading
// false = BLACK line gives LOW reading
//
// Most simple digital IR modules need special handling,
// so for analog sensors we convert the reading below.

bool blackLine = true;


// ======================================================
// SETUP
// ======================================================

void setup() {

  Serial.begin(115200);

  // Motor pins
  pinMode(ENA, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(IN1, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(IN2, OUTPUT);

  pinMode(ENB, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(IN3, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(IN4, OUTPUT);

  // Sensor pins
  for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    pinMode(sensorPins[i], INPUT);
  }

  // Stop motors initially
  stopMotors();

  delay(2000);

  Serial.println("PID Line Follower Started");
}


// ======================================================
// MAIN LOOP
// ======================================================

void loop() {

  // ----------------------------------------------------
  // 1. READ LINE POSITION
  // ----------------------------------------------------

  int position = readLinePosition();


  // ----------------------------------------------------
  // 2. CALCULATE ERROR
  // ----------------------------------------------------

  // Center position = 2000

  error = position - 2000;


  // ----------------------------------------------------
  // 3. PID
  // ----------------------------------------------------

  // Proportional
  float P = error;


  // Integral
  integral += error;

  // Anti-windup
  if (integral > 5000)
    integral = 5000;

  if (integral < -5000)
    integral = -5000;


  // Derivative
  derivative = error - previousError;


  // PID correction
  correction =
      (Kp * P) +
      (Ki * integral) +
      (Kd * derivative);


  // Save error
  previousError = error;


  // ----------------------------------------------------
  // 4. MOTOR SPEED
  // ----------------------------------------------------

  int leftSpeed;
  int rightSpeed;


  /*
     If error is positive:

     Line is towards RIGHT.

     Slow down RIGHT / speed up LEFT
     so the robot turns RIGHT.
  */

  leftSpeed  = baseSpeed + correction;
  rightSpeed = baseSpeed - correction;


  // ----------------------------------------------------
  // 5. LIMIT SPEED
  // ----------------------------------------------------

  leftSpeed = constrain(leftSpeed, minSpeed, maxSpeed);
  rightSpeed = constrain(rightSpeed, minSpeed, maxSpeed);


  // ----------------------------------------------------
  // 6. DRIVE MOTORS
  // ----------------------------------------------------

  driveMotors(leftSpeed, rightSpeed);


  // ----------------------------------------------------
  // DEBUG
  // ----------------------------------------------------

  Serial.print("Position: ");
  Serial.print(position);

  Serial.print("  Error: ");
  Serial.print(error);

  Serial.print("  P: ");
  Serial.print(P);

  Serial.print("  I: ");
  Serial.print(integral);

  Serial.print("  D: ");
  Serial.print(derivative);

  Serial.print("  Correction: ");
  Serial.print(correction);

  Serial.print("  L: ");
  Serial.print(leftSpeed);

  Serial.print("  R: ");
  Serial.println(rightSpeed);


  // Small delay
  delay(5);
}


// ======================================================
// READ LINE POSITION
// ======================================================

int readLinePosition() {

  long weightedSum = 0;
  long sensorSum = 0;


  for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {

    int raw = analogRead(sensorPins[i]);


    /*
       Convert sensor reading so:

       WHITE = approximately 0
       BLACK = approximately high

       If your sensor behaves opposite, change this.
    */

    int value;

    if (blackLine) {

      // For sensors where BLACK produces lower reading
      value = 1023 - raw;

    } else {

      value = raw;
    }


    // Ignore tiny noise
    if (value < 50) {
      value = 0;
    }


    // Position weights
    int weight = i * 1000;


    weightedSum += (long)value * weight;
    sensorSum += value;
  }


  // ----------------------------------------------------
  // LINE LOST
  // ----------------------------------------------------

  if (sensorSum == 0) {

    /*
       If line disappears, keep using the
       previous direction.

       This prevents the robot from immediately
       driving straight off the track.
    */

    if (previousError < 0) {
      return 0;
    } else {
      return 4000;
    }
  }


  // ----------------------------------------------------
  // WEIGHTED AVERAGE
  // ----------------------------------------------------

  int position = weightedSum / sensorSum;


  return position;
}


// ======================================================
// MOTOR CONTROL
// ======================================================

void driveMotors(int leftSpeed, int rightSpeed) {

  // ----------------------------------------------------
  // LEFT MOTOR FORWARD
  // ----------------------------------------------------

  digitalWrite(IN1, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(IN2, LOW);

  analogWrite(ENA, leftSpeed);


  // ----------------------------------------------------
  // RIGHT MOTOR FORWARD
  // ----------------------------------------------------

  digitalWrite(IN3, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(IN4, LOW);

  analogWrite(ENB, rightSpeed);
}


// ======================================================
// STOP MOTORS
// ======================================================

void stopMotors() {

  analogWrite(ENA, 0);
  analogWrite(ENB, 0);

  digitalWrite(IN1, LOW);
  digitalWrite(IN2, LOW);

  digitalWrite(IN3, LOW);
  digitalWrite(IN4, LOW);
}
```

### How the sensor-position calculation works

Suppose the five sensors give approximately:

```text
S1 = 0
S2 = 200
S3 = 800
S4 = 200
S5 = 0
```

The calculation is:

[
Position =
\frac{
S1(0)+S2(1000)+S3(2000)+S4(3000)+S5(4000)
}{
S1+S2+S3+S4+S5
}
]

So:

[
Position =
\frac{0+200000+1600000+600000+0}{1200}
]

[
Position \approx 2000
]

Therefore:

```text
Position = 2000
Error = Position - 2000
Error = 0
```

The robot is centered, so PID produces almost no correction.

This weighted-average method gives fractional/smooth positions instead of simply saying "sensor 2" or "sensor 3," which makes steering smoother. ([GitHub][1])

### Example: line moves right

Suppose:

```text
S1 = 0
S2 = 0
S3 = 300
S4 = 800
S5 = 200
```

The calculated position becomes somewhere around **2800–3000**.

Therefore:

```text
Error = 2900 - 2000
Error = +900
```

PID sees a positive error and changes the motor speeds:

```text
LEFT  = 130 + correction
RIGHT = 130 - correction
```

The left motor becomes faster and the right motor slower, causing the robot to turn toward the right.

The basic PID calculation is:

[
\boxed{Correction=K_pE+K_i\sum E+K_d(E-E_{previous})}
]

which is the same structure used in typical Arduino PID line-following implementations. ([Arduino Project Hub][2])

### ⚠️ One important thing

The **exact `Kp`, `Ki`, and `Kd` values cannot be copied universally**. They depend on your sensor spacing, motors, wheel size, robot weight, battery voltage, and track. PID gains are normally tuned experimentally. ([GitHub][1])

For your first test, I'd use:

```cpp
Kp = 0.08;
Ki = 0.0000;
Kd = 0.80;

baseSpeed = 130;
```

**Don't tune `Ki` yet.** Start with PD. Once it follows the line reliably, we can tune `Kp` and `Kd`, then add a tiny `Ki` only if the robot has a persistent offset.

If your robot is the **same one from our previous parts list**, with the **5 IR sensors + Arduino + L298N + two TT gear motors**, this code is the version I'd start with.

